  <title>Faxing</title>

  <p>PyGtk Posting faxes through the Brother fax machine's <em>BRFAX</em> print
  queue on this computer. The queue needs Brother's fax driver
  (brmfcfaxdrv). Every fax carries its number with it, so the number is always
  asked for before anything is dialed.</p>

  <section id="number">
  <title>The number to dial</title>
  <p>The fax driver takes digits and commas only. A comma is a pause, so
  <code>9,13155551234</code> dials 9 for an outside line first. Long distance
  numbers start with 1. When a contact has a fax number on file it is offered
  already cleaned up, with the number as it was typed shown beside it.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="send_fax">
  <title>Sending any document</title>
  <steps>
    <item><p>Open General > Send fax.</p></item>
    <item><p>Choose a contact, or type the number to dial.</p></item>
    <item><p>Click Add PDFs to choose the documents. They are faxed in the
    order listed.</p></item>
    <item><p>Leave Cover sheet checked to send a cover page with a subject and
    a note, or uncheck it to send only the documents.</p></item>
    <item><p>Click Preview to see exactly what will be sent, then Send
    fax.</p></item>
  </steps>
  </section>

  <section id="invoice">
  <title>Faxing an invoice</title>
  <p>In the invoice window choose Fax invoice... before posting, and check the
  number against the invoice. The menu item then shows the number, and the
  invoice is faxed to it as soon as it is posted. Choose Fax invoice... again to
  change the number, or click Don't fax.</p>
  <p>An invoice already posted can be faxed again from the Unpaid Invoices
  window: right click it and choose Fax invoice.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="other_programs">
  <title>Faxing from other programs</title>
  <p>Brother's fax driver never asks for a number, so a document printed to
  BRFAX from another program normally disappears without being faxed. To fax
  from any program, have the queue hold what is printed to it, by running this
  once in a terminal:</p>
  <screen>sudo lpadmin -p BRFAX -o job-hold-until-default=indefinite</screen>
  <p>After that, while PyGtk Posting is open, anything printed to BRFAX opens
  the Send fax window within a few seconds, with the pages printed. Print more,
  from the same program or another, and they are added to the same fax: print
  only the pages you need from a manual, then the next pages, then the
  invoice, and send them all as one fax. Pages chosen in the print dialog are
  the pages faxed.</p>
  <list>
    <item><p>Remove takes a printed document out of the fax and cancels it in
    the print queue.</p></item>
    <item><p>Closing the window asks before canceling the printed documents
    still waiting.</p></item>
    <item><p>Anything printed while PyGtk Posting is closed waits in the print
    queue, and opens the Send fax window the next time PyGtk Posting
    starts.</p></item>
  </list>
  <p>To stop holding, run:</p>
  <screen>sudo lpadmin -p BRFAX -o job-hold-until-default=no-hold</screen>
  <note><p>Only run one copy of PyGtk Posting while faxing from other programs.
  Each copy opens its own Send fax window for the same pages.</p></note>
  </section>

  <section id="report">
  <title>Did the fax go through?</title>
  <p>PyGtk Posting can only tell you the fax machine took the fax into its
  memory. Busy lines, redials and failed calls are known to the fax machine
  alone. Its transmission report says whether the fax went through; set the
  machine to print one for every fax while you are getting used to faxing from
  PyGtk Posting.</p>
  </section>

